WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.560
One of the things that sets esports apart, is that it's truly global.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.400
Still, pro players are more common in some places than others.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:17.560
"I love my country. I'm coming from the country that made me what I am today."

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.120
"So I want to give something back."

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:34.360
(Casting)

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:40.400
Ladislav “GuardiaN” Kovács managed to make his way into the elite of Counter-Strike’s

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.160
competitive community from a small European country mostly unknown in the scene.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.280
And he did it through his sheer dedication to the game.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:01:01.720
(Casting)

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:06.360
But despite playing with some of the best players the game has ever seen, and holding

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.520
his own as one of the most versatile and effective AWPers ever,

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:19.620
(Casting)

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.740
There is still something GuardiaN has yet to do:

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.580
Win a major.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.200
"There he is, the world's best sniper."

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.880
Born in 1991, GuardiaN grew up in Slovakia.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.000
At age 10, his brother’s copy of Counter-Strike found its way into the home, and it was instantly

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.300
something that GuardiaN understood.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.600
"In 2001, it was all 1.3."

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:56.880
"And the old estate map. When my brother brough CS home, I started playing the game 1v10"

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.560
"The hardest bots. And I felt like I could shoot I could do everything I want."

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:09.760
Over the next eight years, GuardiaN would continue to play 1.6, but also tried Counter-Strike: Source.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.120
All the while, he was still living in Slovakia and playing with domestic teams there.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.920
It was during this time that Guardian picked up the AWP.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:25.680
And if it wasn’t for the fact that he was playing in a smaller region, he may not have become an AWPer at all.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.820
"I began sniping just because our city didn't have a solid sniper that could actually help us."

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:37.360
"So that's the story behind it, and I'm happy it happened that way."

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:43.440
During this period his first roster that competed at an international LAN was Reason Gaming,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.440
a combined Czech and Slovak roster.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:53.200
His first CS:Source LAN event was Lan79, a recurring French tournament, where some thought

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.600
the team was cheating.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:00.920
But GuardiaN, just under a year into his time with the game, was still playing like it was 1.6

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.140
"Ah you know coming from 1.6 you just want to play the game the same way like you want to wallbang

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.500
and stuff like this so."

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.780
GuardiaN made the jump to CS:GO after it became clear that the title would be embraced by

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.160
the competitive community: even if their initial reception was less than cordial.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:24.380
"The other three like Luckey Ted, zid. All three of them weren't convinced that CS:GO was going to be big."

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.680
"And I don't think any... All three of those players didn't like the game."

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.160
He still didn’t love the game, and even considered switching away from the AWP, given

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.680
the changes to the weapon.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:41.460
"I hated the game completely, I don't know why I didn't like it. Too much trash on the map, like chickens

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:42.460
and stuff so."

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.120
Despite eventually coming around to the game, GuardiaN spent his early career in GO playing

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.440
playing on mostly regional teams, including the Czech/Slovakian mix team MyDGB.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.760
Now working a job at Hewlett-Packard, GuardiaN had to find time for the game, even bringing

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:06.960
his PC to work during a night shift so he could play a qualifier for the first IEM Katowice tournament.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:14.800
"It was risky. We had a team of five people, like shift. And my guys were really nice and they knew

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:22.740
I'm playing CS competitively. The shift captain told me, yeah I'm allowed to, so they didn't tell anyone

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:23.760
that I've done it."

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:30.760
GuardiaN’s first big break outside his region would come with Virtus.Pro in June 2013.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:37.600
At the time, VP was a Russian and Ukrainian mixed team and GuardiaN didn’t speak any Russian.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.819
He was forced to learn as he went, and credits Fox with helping him.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.760
A month after GuardiaN’s addition, ANGE1 and Dosia left for Astana Dragons.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:53.760
GuardiaN was on the move again, but had made enough of an impact outside his home region to get noticed.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.420
He was chosen as the 10th best player of 2013 by HLTV.org.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.660
GuardiaN’s AWPing style was versatile, focused around his ability to flick with the AWP whether

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:09.560
he was holding an angle defensively, or peeking aggressively.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.980
(Casting)

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.520
"Everytime I wanted to buy awp, I buy the awp even if I have no armour."

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:24.120
Another hallmark of his play is his uncanny ability to clutch even in terrible situations.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:30.900
(Casting)

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:35.560
After playing on a mix team with Edward, who had also been part of Astana Dragons,

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.520
GuardiaN was invited to join Natus Vincere.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:44.140
GuardiaN had spent a long time in his home region and had only a short-lived stint with VP.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.980
But Na`Vi would end up being his home for years to come.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:56.340
Also on the roster was Zeus, the team’s in-game leader renowned for his slow and methodical

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.920
approach to the game.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.440
(Casting)

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.900
That strategy gave GuardiaN plenty of time to find picks while the team played

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:06.740
around him.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:14.020
"He was very good at reading opponents. And he had the balls to call tactics even 50 seconds left."

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.860
The team’s Major debut was at ESL Major Series One: Katowice, but they finished

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.000
13th-16th after a full month of bootcamp.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.080
But an immediate opportunity to rebound presented itself.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.639
Fnatic had declined their spot to StarLadder Starseries IX based on the ongoing crisis

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:39.440
in Ukraine, but the tournament would be huge for the resurgence of the Na`Vi squad.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:45.420
They didn’t drop a single map as the team defeated NiP twice, and Titan.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.180
It was exactly the sort of win that the new roster needed.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.680
Suddenly, they were on the map, and GuardiaN’s style broke into the mainstream.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:07:00.720
"It feels always kind of special because it was my first bigger event."

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:11.620
"I think back then it was like 100k or 150k. So yeah, it feels special and still I feel the CIS fans and everything

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.600
like that, they stayed behind me and cheered for me so yeah."

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:22.180
In the run-up to the next 2014 Major in Cologne they looked like they could do real damage.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:29.620
(Casting)

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:35.180
But after getting out of groups as the second seed, they ran directly into Fnatic.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:41.320
That series featured furious action, but Na`Vi went out in a narrow 16-14 on Nuke.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:46.640
"Fnatic go through after a hard fought battle, all three maps going to Nuke.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:52.220
Although Na`Vi were firmly in the conversation as a contender, it was over three months until

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.760
they got another shot at a Valve Major.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:08:01.600
"I don't know it was always a close matchup, even in Cologne we lost 2-1 to Fnatic, 16-14 last map."

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:07.960
"So we always missed something. I hope it will be better this time and we will show better results."

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:12.880
Na`Vi were eliminated in the semifinals by eventual winners LDLC.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:20.460
(Casting)

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.380
The  next few months were quiet for the team in

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:30.920
terms of competition until the next Major in March 2015 at ESL One Katowice.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:35.440
After again working their way out of groups, the team fell to the same french players as

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.800
they did in Cologne: this time, representing Team EnVyUs.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:46.580
"I'm glad we got to see the Dust2 that came out of Na'Vi was inspirational. And GuardiaN, such a key figure."

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:52.280
GuardiaN was the only member of Na`Vi to be positive overall for the series, and the team

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.300
went out in the quarterfinals.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:01.260
They had shown some positive potential but so far had only broken the Top 4 at a Major once.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:06.260
Outside of Valve majors the team had only seen significant success only at the most recent

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:09.540
three StarLadder events and at DreamHack Summer.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:13.980
After the tournament, starix stepped down from the team and became a coach, analyst

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.140
and a manager.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.100
Flamie joined as a stand-in before becoming a permanent member.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.940
While GuardiaN had often been relied upon to take a starring role, Flamie added firepower

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.240
to the team that gave him more room to work with.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:33.000
Despite the team’s inconsistent results, GuardiaN was becoming a hallmark name, synonymous

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.120
with excellence with the AWP.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:38.960
(Casting)

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.780
He, along with French AWPer KennyS, were the two names on the tip of everyone’s tongue

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.260
when asked about the game’s best snipers.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.360
But the two had very different approaches to the game.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.780
(Casting)

00:09:54.780 --> 00:10:03.460
"Ahh but there is no doubt he's always been among the top three best awpers in the world."

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:04.920
"With like me"

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.800
The middle of 2015 again saw mixed results.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:15.240
Na`Vi took ESL Pro League 1 and StarSeries XIII from both of France’s best rosters,

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.960
and won ESWC 2015, though some notable rosters
were not attending.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:27.860
(Casting)

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:32.700
France had its revenge at ESL One Cologne, the next Valve major.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:38.400
After the group stage, Na`Vi had to play against EnvyUs in the quarterfinals:

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.720
Kenny and GuardiaN facing off.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:57.560
(Casting)

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:02.060
And though the maps were close, they lost 2-0.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:11.180
It was another Top 8 appearance for Na`Vi where they had not met their own expectations.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:16.280
Over the next few months they managed Top 4 appearances at a bevy of events but didn’t

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:17.020
win any.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:22.400
Still, their consistency suggested DreamHack Open Cluj-Napoca 2015, the upcoming Valve

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.280
Major, might be their best chance yet to take it all.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:32.840
(Casting)

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:39.060
Even in the early stages of this tournament, nV and Kenny were there to threaten Na`Vi.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:43.640
In the same group, Na`Vi lost their match against the french side but still made it

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:45.840
out of the group as second-seed.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.920
The path to the grand finals was not easy.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:54.260
Luminosity Gaming, the best Brazilian team, were increasingly showing they were top-tier

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:59.160
competitors, and Ninjas in Pyjamas stood in the way in the semifinals.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:06.260
But once they had fought all the way to grand finals, it was Team EnVyUs again standing in their way.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:10.520
"And this is just a legendary lineup going against another legendary lineup, two legendary lineups going at it,

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:16.180
and two legendary AWPers going at it. And today we'll see one of those AWPers who's never won a Major

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:17.840
before, take the title."

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:23.720
Na`Vi’s dominance on their map pick of Train was immediately challenged by nV,

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:29.480
And the French side took it 16-14, including a heartbreaking 1v2 by Happy.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:37.200
(Casting)

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:44.400
Na`Vi now had to somehow win on Cobble, a map they, for all intents and purposes, didn’t play

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.160
They couldn’t push it to a third map.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:56.880
"And there it is, nV winning the last Major of 2015 and the second final in a row here for three of these players."

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:58.920
The first for Kenny and Apex."

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:07.360
A 16-5 stomp saw GuardiaN get the closest he had ever been to a championship and then

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:08.680
fall short.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:14.960
"Of course it hurts to lose a final like that because next map was Cobble, and we never practiced Cobble before."

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.920
This was the major GuardiaN could have won.

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.640
"It just hurts, when you see how you lose the final."

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:30.780
"Of course if you're a professional player you're thinking about it, you have the memories from it so yeah."

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.720
As the numbers showed, he wasn’t the problem.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:39.360
GuardiaN was +12 across both maps, better than any other player in the server besides

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.220
Kenny.

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.920
He was also selected as the tournament MVP.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:47.360
(Casting)

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:54.280
As the dust settled from Cluj, the team had an incredible rebound performance at IEM San Jose

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:56.720
though the event was an invitational.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:03.520
"I'm speechless like, this was the first time that we won like a team, we were playing as a team."

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.900
"I think we did a great job."

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:10.900
The team was now consistently making finals, and a major win seemed possible.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:14.520
(Casting)

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:22.840
In just over two months in early 2016, the team raked in over $150,000 in prize money.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:29.100
At Counter Pit League S2 Finals, GuardiaN destroyed the competition, posting unbelievable

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:32.200
stats in the grand final against Astralis.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:38.360
So when MLG Major Championship Columbus rolled around, Na`Vi were again in the discussion

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:39.620
as one of the favorites.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:43.520
But already, the odds were stacked against GuardiaN.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:53.600
"I think injury was there like for a month. Before Counter-Pit I mean. And I never realised that it could be bad."

00:14:53.600 --> 00:15:02.460
"Sometimes I had pain in the wrist but I always thought, exhaustion like I got exhausted in my arm or something."

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.600
I used to play a lot of CS back then."

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:09.460
The injury had been ongoing, but GuardiaN ignored the problem, thinking it

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:11.920
was just normal wear-and-tear.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:16.320
Though the team managed to top their group and defeat NiP and Astralis without dropping

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:21.800
a map, the injury had flared up by the grand finals series against Luminosity.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:26.260
GuardiaN, trying to compensate for his reduced range of movement in his wrist,

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.560
cranked up the mouse sensitivity settings.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:29.760
But it wasn’t enough,

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:35.420
(Casting)

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:40.580
and GuardiaN had one of the worst performances of his career in an event final.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:45.040
"Story of the tournament so far, you've got GuardiaN, he hasn't shown up

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:46.920
Someone needs to get on the phone and ask where he is."

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:50.720
The Major title again slipped away.

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:58.880
In the aftermath, despite some early concerns, a doctor did eventually clear him to play

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.920
at DreamHack Malmo, the next big event for the team.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:07.400
"GuardiaN his wrist, his mouse hand, has been injured. He had an inflamed bone or something in his wrist."

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:14.260
"But apparently he's been cleared by the doctors to attend the event, but it is some strain on himself."

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.060
They took second.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:21.260
The next Valve Major for Na`Vi was in Cologne and barely three months from the previous

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:22.940
one in Columbus.

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:28.400
But instead of another finals appearance, they went out 5th-8th with a shocking loss

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:31.200
to Team Liquid and their new talent, s1mple.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:34.220
(Casting)

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:41.260
By August 2016 Na’Vi had decided to remove Zeus and add s1mple, but the change worsened

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:45.200
divisions in the team that had appeared earlier
in the year.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:54.760
"I think like after we kicked Zeus, I think the change of lineup didn't happen the way it should happen."

00:16:54.760 --> 00:17:00.100
Though s1mple was an incredible talent, there were questions about whether the change was what the team

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:01.060
needed.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:07.400
While the space between the last two Majors had been short, it would be nearly a half a year before the next.

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:13.740
In the interim, both Na`Vi and GuardiaN managed a career milestone: winning a big premier

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:17.120
event with a $250,000 prize pool.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:23.800
(Casting)

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:29.520
At ESL One New York, Na`Vi went undefeated in the Swiss group and then defeated Team

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.240
Liquid and Virtus.Pro in the bracket to take first.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:37.680
GuardiaN, overcome by emotion, refused to hide it.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:42.560
"I've been with you a long time, we've known each other for over a dozen years, I know how much this means to

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:43.060
you."

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.000
"Can you put it in words for your fans?"

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:51.680
"I can't. I'm just speechless. It feels so f***ing, so good."

00:17:51.760 --> 00:18:00.840
"The thing is. Why I was super emotional was because, I've been dedicated to CS 24/7,

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:10.760
I chose CS over friends, I chose CS over family, over everything. And thinking about I lost so many finals,

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:19.520
and then finally winning one, it changed a lot. You think about it like, finally the dedication showed

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:21.800
I can win tournaments like that so."

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:28.600
Though it was a huge moment for GuardiaN and his team, it would be the last big win for the roster

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:30.200
before his departure.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.840
The win hadn’t healed the cracks in the foundation of the team.

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:44.640
Na`Vi didn’t make Top 4 at either the ELEAGUE Major: Atlanta 2017 or the PGL Major Krakow.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:52.160
Their only placement of note in the first half of 2017 was at the Adrenaline Cyber League, a CIS-region tournament

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:54.960
where they placed second among four teams.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:19:00.160
"Jet lagged, tired, they still did it. I mean impressive performance from VP you got to give it to them."

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:01.620
"Congratulations to the guys."

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:07.040
After Krakow, both the team and GuardiaN were
faced with hard decisions about the future

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:08.040
of the roster.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:12.820
Na`Vi had been a home for GuardiaN for three and a half years.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:17.960
And during that time he had clearly shown his skill as one of the world’s best AWPers,

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:27.360
(Casting)

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:32.140
But it was also clear that there were other opportunities elsewhere, and the internal

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:35.320
divisions in the team weren’t going to disappear.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:44.100
"Na'Vi gave me everything when I came into the team. They gave me the kind of family, they asked me to stay"

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:52.180
"But I had some issues, that I couldn't accept and they chose someone else over me so."

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:55.680
GuardiaN’s next team shocked the CS community.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:03.020
In August 2017 he joined FaZe Clan, and the team acquired Olofmeister shortly thereafter.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:08.310
They joined rain, karrigan and NiKo to form a multi-national roster that wasn’t hard

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.040
to call a superteam.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:15.540
"Did you ever see yourself playing on the same teamwith Olofmeister on top of with NiKo and now with Rain

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:16.400
on one team?"

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:17.420
"Did you ever imagine that would happen?"

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:21.640
"No to be honest, that's something I would have never imagined."

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:27.220
The consistency shown by this roster far-outstripped the Na`Vi squad he had left.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:32.800
Though their debut at DreamHack Masters Malmo 2017 was a disappointment, they didn’t let

00:20:32.809 --> 00:20:33.840
that get to them.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:39.320
Instead, they doubled down on hard work: something that GuardiaN was very familiar with.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:45.100
(Casting)

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:50.040
And the first major with FaZe saw GuardiaN reach yet another grand final.

00:20:50.160 --> 00:21:08.440
(Casting)

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:15.600
At Boston, it was GuardiaN dominating in the grand finals series against Cloud9.

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:31.880
(Casting

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:37.720
He was the only player positive on his team in the final.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:41.640
The action was maybe the greatest of any Major final.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:50.920
(Casting)

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:54.660
But again, it wasn’t GuardiaN’s Major to win.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:22:10.400
(Casting)

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:19.040
The team’s dedication to being the best means that GuardiaN believes that FaZe will

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:22.600
be the last team he plays with before he retires.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:26.680
"Sooner or later I think it will come, and FaZe Clan will be the last team."

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:33.040
He’s already shown that he is very much in form, and FaZe remains a very real threat.

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:39.400
(Casting)

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:42.960
He will have other chances to win Majors, surely.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:45.760
But what if he doesn’t?

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:53.480
"Well if I don't win one before I retire from competitive CS, I think it will be very sad."

00:22:53.480 --> 00:23:00.180
"Having individual achievements like being the best player in the world is something that is least important

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:06.560
in your career because this is a team game, I don't care if you're the best or not the best, you care about the team

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:07.240
winnings."

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:13.280
Still, whether or not he wins a Major, GuardiaN has been a part of many of the greatest moments

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:16.620
in CS:GO, and created plenty of his own.

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:22.660
From a small competitive region to the biggest stages in Counter-Strike history, GuardiaN’s

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:29.500
path to excellence was forged by sheer perseverance and some of the greatest AWPing the game

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:30.500
has ever seen.

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:31.820
(Casting)

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:35.300
"And he's got all five!"

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:39.900
"There he is, the world's best sniper!"

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:47.220
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

